-# v0.27.0-alpha6 research reconciliation architecture baseline
-
-## Alpha6 reconciliation boundary
-
-Research is attached to a logical broadcast, never to every physical file candidate independently. Reconciliation therefore groups by durable researched-broadcast identity before any user-facing decision is created. Physical recordings, alternate encodes and machine-local file locations may supply evidence, but they do not multiply the number of research decisions.
-
-Automatic reconciliation is deliberately narrow. It may apply saved research only when one deterministic show/date/broadcast-slot/part identity exists, or dismiss candidate noise when that research is already attached to an available broadcast. Ambiguous same-day slots, conflicting identities and low-confidence candidates remain manual. Automatic scalar updates fill blanks only; additive people, topics and Moments use the established guarded merge rules.
-
-Schema 38 stores whether a candidate truly requires review, its category, the recommended action and whether the final decision was automatic or manual. The reconciliation action ledger supports inspection and guarded undo. An undone automatic match enters a manual hold so repeated triage cannot silently recreate it.
-
-Research quality findings, missing-broadcast preservation and media duplicate/integrity concerns remain separate workflows. The Research UI reports decisions a person can actually resolve; Archive Health may link to those decisions but does not turn informational file relationships into research approval tasks.
-
-Alpha6 remains non-destructive and preserves the alpha5 broadcast identity of show + date + broadcast slot + part. No audio path or recording is moved, renamed, quarantined or deleted.
-
-# v0.27.0-alpha5 archive identity architecture baseline
-
-## Alpha5 archive identity boundary
-
-The durable broadcast identity is show + date + broadcast slot + part. Physical media paths remain separate records. A rescan may improve the parsed identity or relink a new machine-local path without replacing playback, research or transcript knowledge attached to the broadcast.
-
-Folder assignment is a fallback, not stronger evidence than an explicit show name in a filename. `OpieRadio Edition` is retained as the user-recognisable slot label but no longer occupies the recording-edition field. Full-file hashes are exact-copy evidence; partial hashes, sizes and durations are candidate evidence only.
-
-Backup files still contain a complete database for same-PC recovery. On a different computer, restore preserves destination library roots and makes imported paths historical/unavailable until a local scan relinks them. This is an interim portable-location model ahead of the fuller recording/location federation planned for v0.28/v0.29.
-
-
-## Alpha3 local-worker boundary
-
-`TranscriptSpeakerCluster` is the durable anonymous identity produced by diarization. `voice_people` links a user-confirmed cluster to a canonical person, while `voice_samples` and `voice_profiles` remain local machine-learning evidence. WPF only presents assignment and status; matching, profile aggregation and engine contracts stay inside `TheRadioVault.Transcription`. Transcript package exchange carries labels and assignments but never exports local embeddings.
-
-The stable v0.26 Core/Data/Media/Services/Research/Web/WPF boundaries remain intact. `TheRadioVault.Transcription` now includes a concrete whisper.cpp adapter behind `ITranscriptionEngine`; WPF owns only local executable/model preferences, downloads and presentation. Database schema 37 preserves the reproducible worker options and adds per-segment content/review state without changing the proven playback, research or web ownership systems.
-
-
-## Alpha4 quality and exchange boundary
-
-`TranscriptQualityProcessor` is deterministic and independent of WPF: it classifies only explicit non-speech markers, merges adjacent equal non-speech regions and preserves surrounding timings. `TranscriptMetadataSanitizer` applies an allowlist before portable export. `TranscriptExchangeService` owns format detection, v1/v2 JSON compatibility and v3 ZIP packaging. Archive metadata is converted into a bounded initial vocabulary prompt through `ITranscriptRepository`, keeping show-specific context out of the engine contract itself.
-
-Native-process details remain contained by `WhisperCppTranscriptionEngine`. Model download and Windows file-picking stay in the WPF adapter, while repositories, packages and durable coordination remain platform-neutral. See `TRANSCRIPTION.md`.
-
-# v0.26.0 final architecture baseline
-
-v0.26.0 ships the established Core/Data/Media/Services/Research/Web/WPF boundaries and database schema 33. The unified playback session, guarded research import/rollback and browser-side offline-storage boundaries are now the stable v0.26 baseline. Future work should preserve these seams unless a documented migration requires otherwise.
-
-Phone support diagnostics receive the current application version through `WebServerOptions.AppVersion`, supplied by the desktop adapter. This removes a hard-coded preview identifier without coupling `TheRadioVault.Web` to WPF or desktop assembly metadata.
-
-## Live audio transport
-
-Playback ownership and media delivery are separate concerns. Ownership remains in the shared playback session; `/audio/{id}` supplies a fresh non-cacheable byte-range resource for each local decoder session.
-
-## Phone takeover claim window (0.26.0-beta1-preview6-buildfix18-phone-transfer-prime-retry-fix-archivist)
-
-An explicit phone takeover introduces a short client-side claim window. While `localTakeoverPending` is true, passive server snapshots cannot classify the phone as inactive. Authoritative ownership is still established only by the accepted forced playback heartbeat.
-
-## Unified playback session (0.26.0-beta1-preview6-buildfix13-unified-playback-ownership-archivist)
-
-Playback is represented by `WebPlaybackSession`: authoritative player state, desktop state, phone state, owner device, owner client and a monotonically increasing generation. Ownership is independent of whether the active output is currently playing or paused. This prevents a paused phone from silently becoming a PC remote and prevents a delayed heartbeat from reclaiming a newer owner.
-
-The active client exposes normal transport controls. An inactive client treats the playhead as read-only and exposes only its custom **bring playback here** centre control. Desktop and phone decoders may follow the shared position while dormant, but only the owner is permitted to produce audio or mutate transport state.
-
-Transfers retain the buildfix12 lock boundary: `_playbackGate` protects only short shared-state mutations, `_desktopCommandGate` serialises WPF transport calls, and no dispatcher call, database write or synchronous event publication occurs while `_playbackGate` is held.
-
-## Playback ownership lock boundary (0.26.0-beta1-preview6-buildfix12-phone-handoff-deadlock-fix-archivist)
-
-`WebArchiveProvider` now uses separate locks for phone playback state and desktop command ordering. The provider must never hold `_playbackGate` while invoking `IWebPlaybackController`, writing playback progress, or publishing synchronous application events. WPF dispatcher work is serialised by `_desktopCommandGate`, allowing desktop pause/save callbacks to re-enter the web adapter safely without a server-thread/UI-thread lock inversion.
-
-## Shared playback ownership (0.26.0-beta1-preview6-buildfix11-automatic-playback-ownership-archivist)
-
-Playback transport remains split cleanly across layers. The WPF shell alone owns the desktop decoder and emits `PlaybackOwnershipChangedEvent` when it becomes audible. `WebArchiveProvider` coordinates desktop remote-control leases, browser playback leases and short explicit phone claims without exposing WPF to the web assembly. The embedded client automatically pauses its local audio when desktop ownership is observed and uses an internal `claim-phone` command when a normal phone Play action starts. No user-facing handoff mode is required.
-
-## Mobile offline boundary
-
-Manual offline media storage is entirely browser-side. `TheRadioVault.Web` exposes byte-range/full audio delivery and a narrow offline-progress mutation; it never manages phone storage. The embedded client uses IndexedDB and Blob URLs. The desktop provider accepts only monotonic resume updates and keeps the SQLite schema unchanged.
-
-# Architecture
-
-The solution is being separated into reusable layers while preserving the working WPF application.
-
-- `TheRadioVault.Core`: platform-neutral domain rules and feature flags.
-- `TheRadioVault.Data`: SQLite connection policy, schema and migrations.
-- `TheRadioVault.Media`: media inspection and playback-adjacent utilities.
-- `TheRadioVault.Services`: reusable application services.
-- `TheRadioVault`: WPF desktop client and embedded LAN host.
-
-New research and web features should place rules outside window code whenever practical. The desktop and web interfaces must consume the same database identities and domain rules.
-
-## Phoenix migration status
-
-Phoenix is an incremental restructuring programme, not a rewrite. The solution must remain buildable and usable after every slice.
-
-Current assemblies already provide useful boundaries for Core, Data, Services, and Media. The first Phoenix slice adds an independent Tests assembly and a mandatory release gate. Later slices will extract web transport/contracts and research-domain services only when each extraction can be completed without duplicating behaviour or changing the database format.
-
-## Phoenix 2 boundaries
-
-### TheRadioVault.Research
-
-Contains pure research-quality inputs, findings and rules. It has no dependency on WPF, SQLite or the desktop project. The desktop `ResearchQualityAuditService` is now an adapter that loads durable research records and maps them into the engine.
-
-### TheRadioVault.Web
-
-Contains the LAN-only HTTP host, request parsing, access-token validation, episode querying, show facets, artwork delivery and byte-range audio streaming. It consumes `IWebArchiveProvider`; the WPF project implements that provider against the current database service.
-
-This direction allows both subsystems to be tested independently and later hosted by clients other than WPF.
-
-## Phoenix 3 API boundary
-
-`TheRadioVault.Web.Contracts.WebApiRoutes` defines the versioned `/api/v1` surface. `LocalWebServer` handles LAN/token enforcement and serialisation, while `IWebArchiveProvider` supplies broadcast details, research, Archive Health, playback state and queue snapshots. The desktop adapter is the only layer that knows about the current SQLite-backed application models.
-
-## Phoenix 4 jobs and events
-
-`TheRadioVault.Core.Events` now owns platform-neutral application event contracts and the in-process `ApplicationEventBus`. Publishers do not know which client will react; WPF subscribers marshal to the dispatcher, while the web adapter translates events into a bounded sequence-numbered change feed.
-
-`TheRadioVault.Services.Jobs` owns `BackgroundJobQueue`. It limits concurrency, keeps compact task history, publishes progress through both a conventional event and the typed event bus, and exposes cooperative cancellation. Scans and other workflows remain responsible for checking the supplied cancellation token at safe points.
-
-`ILivePlaybackStateStore` is an atomic shared snapshot used by the desktop playback shell and the web API. Phoenix 4 reports state only; two-way remote transport control remains intentionally deferred.
-
-Event-driven desktop refreshes are coalesced before they reach WPF, preventing a large import or repair batch from scheduling hundreds of redundant library reloads.
-
-## Phoenix 5 playback boundary
-
-The WPF process remains the owner of the desktop media engine. `IWebPlaybackController` is the only web-facing transport boundary; its WPF implementation marshals commands to the dispatcher. The web provider coordinates controller leases, phone playback leases, queue mutations and change events, but never reads or manipulates WPF controls directly.
-
-The custom browser player uses the versioned API and a hidden native audio element. This preserves platform audio reliability while keeping the visible player client-specific.
-
-## Secure Radio Vault Anywhere transport
-
-When secure offline access is enabled, the Web assembly owns two listeners through the same lifecycle:
-
-- the configured HTTP port serves only token-protected certificate onboarding and redirects ordinary application requests;
-- the configured HTTPS port serves the application shell, versioned API, artwork and byte-range audio through `SslStream` using TLS 1.2/1.3.
-
-The WPF shell owns certificate persistence through `SecureWebCertificateService`. A persistent private local root CA signs renewable server certificates containing the current LAN IPv4 addresses. Only the public root certificate/profile crosses the network. `TheRadioVault.Web` receives the active server certificate and onboarding payloads through `WebServerOptions`; it does not know the certificate-file location or password.
-
-The Service Worker caches only the embedded application shell. Manual audio downloads remain in IndexedDB and are never placed in the HTTP cache or downloaded automatically.
+All output is written to the existing schema-41 `library_truth_*` shadow tables. No live-library adoption is enabled.
